```ADD_PAPER
2509.19326v1
This paper introduces a comprehensive benchmark framework for evaluating automated review generation by LLMs against human experts, using a dataset of 1,683 papers and 6,495 reviews from major AI conferences such as ICLR and NeurIPS. The study rigorously examines how LLMs perform on distinct review aspects—particularly comparing descriptive content (e.g., strengths, methodological summaries) and critical feedback (e.g., detection of weaknesses). Extensive experimental results reveal that while LLMs can capture contributions and methodological details (producing 15.74% more descriptive entities in certain sections), they consistently underperform in critical reasoning; for example, they generate 59.42% fewer critical comments when evaluating weaknesses compared to human reviewers. These findings emphasize that advanced reasoning and prompt engineering techniques are necessary to refine output, suggesting that integrating dynamic, bi-directional prompt adjustments could enhance the detection of nuanced issues in peer reviews. The insights presented in this paper provide a solid experimental foundation for designing a two-stage, bi-directional, author-feedback review and reviewer reward system in AI conferences, where refined metric-based evaluation of review quality is paramount.
``` 
~~~~~~~~~~~
