```ADD_PAPER
2505.11718v2
This paper introduces REMOR, an automated system for generating high-quality peer reviews through a multi-objective reinforcement learning framework that tightly couples reasoning trace extraction with dynamic reward modeling. REMOR leverages structured human feedback to construct detailed, multi-aspect reward signals that guide the generation of reviews, enabling the system to capture nuanced critiques as well as affirmations. Extensive experiments on datasets from top-tier AI conferences (e.g., ICLR and NeurIPS) show that, by iteratively adjusting prompt configurations based on evolving reward signals, REMOR achieves significant improvements in both the descriptiveness and critical accuracy of its generated reviews—sometimes more than doubling the alignment with expert human evaluations compared to standard approaches. Moreover, the study demonstrates that integrating adaptive prompt engineering techniques within a two-stage, bi-directional framework (wherein both reviewer evaluations and author feedback are refined iteratively) can lead to measurable enhancements in the fidelity of review quality metrics. These results provide strong empirical support for designing next-generation peer-review systems that maximize accuracy in assessing review quality and dynamically incentivize both authors and reviewers.
``` 
~~~~~~~~~~~
