PhD Student:  ```DIALOGUE
Hey, I’ve been mulling over your proposed three-part study and I’m excited about the potential. Here’s an alternative yet complementary plan:

1. Data & Keyword Dynamics:  
   • We’ll scrape public OpenReview and arXiv data to track ICLR keyword dynamics (2018–2025) using BERTopic, fine-tuning BERT on our dataset.  
   • Alongside, we want to incorporate token-level prompt adjustments: leveraging a dynamic token-reward mechanism (inspired by TRPrompt) that “reweights” the extracted keywords based on evolving review contexts.

2. Typo Detection & Math-Aware LLM:  
   • Starting with a pretrained T5, we’ll design a math-aware typo detection model. The twist is integrating an entropy-guided, token-level calibration module (from the zero-variance prompt ideas in the “No Prompt Left Behind” work), ensuring that even subtle math-specific errors are caught.  
   • We’ll annotate a small initial dataset from technical paper excerpts, then scale up using semi-supervised learning with iterative fine-tuning.

3. Two-Stage Bi-Directional Review Workflow:  
   • Stage One: Generate a “reference” review capturing strengths, summaries, and guiding questions using strict JSON output prompts.  
   • Stage Two: Produce a critical review focused on weaknesses and ratings by dynamically adapting the prompt based on output from stage one and subsequent author feedback.  
   • We simulate a reviewer reward system where author feedback (collected via pre-defined JSON scoring rubrics) dynamically tunes reviewer impact scores, reinforcing hypotheses like reduced retaliatory scoring (H2) and stable badge thresholds (H3).  
   • Innovatively, we’ll integrate a token-level adjustment during the prompt generation so that any prompt deviations provide a feedback signal for refining future outputs. This is a nod to REMOR’s coupling of detailed reward signals with generation.

For quantitative evaluation, we measure:
• Error rates (typos per 1,000 words) over time, hypothesizing a decline (H1).
• Keyword churn metrics with top-20 dynamics.
• Reviewer impact score variations and badge thresholds across simulated epochs.
• The effect of LLM reference reviews on minimizing low-effort human review behavior (H4).

I propose we build a lightweight sandbox prototype that operates on a controlled subset of public preprints. We can rapidly iterate by running ablation studies: comparing different LLMs (e.g., T5 vs. a fine-tuned BERT variant), with and without token-level reward shaping. This should give us quick, clear signals on the effectiveness of our bi-directional, dynamic prompt adjustments.

What do you think? Any further modifications or clarifications you’d suggest before we start implementing?
``` 
~~~~~~~~~~~
