Postdoc:  ```PLAN
Title: Real-Time Dual-Feedback Peer-Review Quality Pipeline

Overview:
We propose a lightweight sandbox prototype that integrates real-time feedback, dynamic token-level adjustments, and a dual-stage review generation process to quantitatively evaluate and enhance peer-review quality signals in AI conferences. Our approach synergizes three empirical components while leveraging state-of-the-art ML architectures.

Components:

1. Real-Time Modular Quality Monitor:
   • Data Collection: Scrape public peer reviews and metadata from OpenReview and arXiv (focusing on ICLR and related venues from 2018–2025) while strictly adhering to TOS.
   • Model & Technique: Use Sentence-BERT to encode review segments and implement a dynamic reweighting mechanism. Token-level uncertainty is computed via entropy measures, allowing us to re-calibrate keyword significance in real time.
   • Visualization: Build an interactive dashboard that continuously displays top-20 evolving keyword clusters and emergent topics.

2. Hybrid Math-Aware Typo Detection Pipeline:
   • Base Model: Start with a pretrained T5 model.
   • Enhancement: Fuse T5 with a dedicated math-aware transformer (pre-trained on a corpus of technical math texts) using adapter modules.
   • Adaptive Calibration: Integrate an entropy-guided token-level reward adjustment module (inspired by zero-variance prompt methods) to fine-tune sensitivity to subtle mathematical errors.
   • Dataset & Evaluation: Assemble a curated dataset from technical excerpts (annotated manually for typos) and scale up via semi-supervised learning. Quantitatively measure error rates as errors per 1,000 words.

3. Dual-Feedback Review Workflow with Dynamic Incentives:
   • Stage One – Reference Review Generation:
     - Use a strict JSON schema prompt to generate a comprehensive review covering summaries, strengths, and guiding questions.
     - Leverage a fine-tuned BERT variant to validate outputs and re-calibrate prompts via live token-level feedback.
   • Stage Two – Critical Review Generation:
     - Generate a second review focusing on weaknesses and assigning ratings, informed by adjustments from author feedback.
     - Collect structured JSON-based author scores using pre-defined scoring rubrics and compute reviewer impact scores.
   • Simulation: Implement a live incentive algorithm that updates reviewer badges (e.g., top 10%/30%) and flags potential retaliatory bias.
   • Ablation Studies: Compare outcomes between models (T5 vs. fine-tuned BERT) and measure differences with/without token-level reward reweighting.

Quantitative Metrics & Hypotheses:
   • Error Rates: Measure typos per 1,000 words; Hypothesis H1 predicts a decline over successive years.
   • Keyword Dynamics: Top-20 keyword churn and dynamic clustering signals.
   • Reviewer Impact: Monitor stability of badge thresholds and computed reviewer scores; test H2 (reduced retaliatory scoring) and H3 (stable badge thresholds with robust author feedback).
   • Low-Effort Review Deterrence: Evaluate if LLM-generated reference reviews (H4) discourage low-effort human reviews.
   • Additional metric: Track token distribution anomalies during prompt generation and measure the improvement from token-level adjustments.

Experiment Execution:
   1. Rapid first-pass implementation of the end-to-end pipeline in a controlled sandbox environment using a subset of public preprints.
   2. Run ablation experiments to isolate impacts of each module (real-time reweighting, hybrid pipeline, dual-feedback mechanism).
   3. Collect quantitative metrics and visualize outcomes on the interactive dashboard.
   4. Iterate prompt designs and calibration weights based on anomaly flags and metric shifts.

This plan offers an innovative, real-time, and dynamic approach to enhance peer-review quality assessment. By integrating dual-stage review generation with live author feedback and adaptive prompt engineering, we aim to push the boundaries of automated peer-review evaluation systems.
  
Next Steps:
   • Develop and deploy the prototype in a small-scale sandbox trial.
   • Analyze the performance using the quantitative metrics and conduct iterative refinements.
   • Pre-register core hypotheses and prepare for a broader experimental deployment.

End of Plan.
  
Submitting this blueprint for immediate implementation and iterative evaluation.
